<LearningGuideLayout
  title="How to Set Up Your Godot Game World in Blender"
  description="Author a Godot level's sky color, gravity, background music, and load or unload events from Blender with Folded Paper Engine."
  summary="A level is more than its meshes. It has a mood, a sense of weight, a soundtrack, and an entrance. FPE lets the Blender scene describe those world-level decisions alongside the art that inspired them."
  relatedSlugs={[
    "play-3d-audio-godot",
    "change-scenes-load-levels-godot",
    "build-levels-in-blender-for-godot",
  ]}
  fpeHeading="What FPE creates in Godot"
>
  <div class="guide-summary">
    <strong>You will make:</strong> a Blender-authored level that arrives in Godot
    with its intended sky, gravity, music, and lifecycle events.
  </div>
  <GuideFigure
    src="/assets/images/guides/blender/scene-settings-panel.png"
    alt="FPE Scene Settings open in Blender with sky gravity and music controls"
    caption="Scene-wide settings live in Blender's Scene Properties, inside the Folded Paper Engine panel."
  />
  <h2>Open the FPE Scene panel in Blender</h2><p>
    Every setting in the next two sections is authored in Blender's <strong
      >Scene → Folded Paper Engine</strong
    > panels—not in Godot's Inspector. These settings belong to the Blender scene,
    not an individual prop. Start with a small level that already exports successfully;
    world settings should enrich a proven workflow rather than obscure a broken one.
  </p>
  <h2>Set the world mood in Blender</h2><ol>
    <li>
      In the FPE Scene panel, choose <strong>Sky Color</strong> to establish the
      level's background tone. Treat it as a starting environment color, then refine
      lighting and materials in context.
    </li><li>
      In that same Blender panel, set <strong>Gravity</strong> in meters per second
      squared. Use familiar gravity unless floatiness or heaviness is intentional.
    </li><li>
      Choose <strong>Background Music</strong> using the path the audio resource
      will have inside the Godot project.
    </li><li>
      Set <strong>Background Music Volume</strong> in decibels. Preview the actual
      mix after import in Godot rather than choosing by number alone.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <GuideFigure
    src="/assets/images/guides/blender/sub-scene-panel.png"
    alt="FPE Sub Scene panel configured for a cottage interior"
    caption="Use Sub Scene settings when an interior or adjoining area needs its own loading boundary."
  />
  <h2>Give the level an entrance and exit</h2><p>
    <strong>Scene Load Events</strong> run commands as the level becomes active.
    Use them for deliberate initialization such as starting an animation, dispatching
    a named event, or establishing a camera beat. <strong
      >Scene Unload Events</strong
    > are for cleanup or a final transition action. Keep both lists short and meaningful;
    complex persistent state still belongs to Godot systems.
  </p>
  <GuideFigure
    src="/assets/images/guides/godot/cookie-beans-runtime.png"
    alt="A complete FPE level running in Godot"
    caption="Verify atmosphere, collision, player start, sound, and transitions together in the running Godot level."
  />
  <h2>Verify the whole level</h2><ul>
    <li>The sky matches the intended mood under the actual camera.</li><li>
      Physics props fall and jump forces feel appropriate under the selected
      gravity.
    </li><li>
      Music resolves to a valid Godot resource path, begins once, and stops
      according to the level's ownership.
    </li><li>Load events do not fire twice during development reloading.</li><li
    >
      Unload events do not reference nodes that have already been removed.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <Fragment slot="fpe"
    ><p>
      FPE reads scene-level properties from the GLB, establishes the
      corresponding Godot environment and gravity, queues background music, and
      dispatches the authored load/unload command lists. Artists can own the
      level's mood and opening beat; engineers remain responsible for global
      save state, cross-level systems, and any rules that must outlive the
      scene.
    </p><p>
      <a href="/docs/fpe-scene-settings.html"
        >Read the complete FPE scene settings reference</a
      >.
    </p></Fragment
  >
</LearningGuideLayout>
